[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi all i found this <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/Holder.html\">HolderWith</a> where r is not  restricted to (0, 1] but ℝ≥0. However, the textbook convention differ for r &gt; 1:  since r &gt; 1 forces f to be constant see a simple proof here: <br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1906x1792\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/aYGkZSd76uoPe1H5igKoK_qz/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/aYGkZSd76uoPe1H5igKoK_qz/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 615503696,
        "sender_full_name": "Zixiao Jolene Wang",
        "timestamp": 1786313308
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>What is the textbook definition for r &gt; 1, if it is different from Mathlib's?</p>",
        "id": 615505341,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1786314453
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If the textbook says \"we don't consider r &gt; 1 because it is useless\", I think Mathlib is fine as-is, similar to how a lot of definitions accept input outside intended usage and output junk value. We can add a theorem highlighting the fact about forcing constant function, though</p>",
        "id": 615506104,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1786315378
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You are talking about functions <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]\\to\\R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span></span></span></span> in your LLM question, and mathlib is talking about functions between (a generalization of) metric spaces, where you can certainly have nonconstant functions and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1.</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&gt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.</span></span></span></span></p>",
        "id": 615506494,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1786315746
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>are they at least locally constant then?</p>",
        "id": 615508056,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1786317109
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>HolderWith also works with pseudoemetric, so if the codomain carries the zero pseudoemetric then all functions are Holder for all exponents</p>",
        "id": 615509388,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1786318474
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>whatever the analogue of locally constant is for pseudometric spaces, then</p>",
        "id": 615510270,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1786319223
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>locally... inseparable?</p>",
        "id": 615510304,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1786319259
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>the preimage of the closure of every singleton is open</p>",
        "id": 615510338,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1786319308
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I was basically checking this <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-0348-0387-8\">book</a> and saw the footnote:<br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1418x1222\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/cfCjQPd5hhHoJAdKq30GK_oR/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/cfCjQPd5hhHoJAdKq30GK_oR/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 615528829,
        "sender_full_name": "Zixiao Jolene Wang",
        "timestamp": 1786330822
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Definition.20of.20HolderWith.20with.20r.20.3E.201/near/615506494\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>You are talking about functions <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]\\to\\R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span></span></span></span> in your LLM question, and mathlib is talking about functions between (a generalization of) metric spaces, where you can certainly have nonconstant functions and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1.</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&gt;1.</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.</span></span></span></span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I was thinking about R^d actually, cause for most of the question for statistical purpose (yes it is not the most general one but most research question are using that definition), when the input space is R^d it is always constant R^d, maybe  <a class=\"stream\" data-stream-id=\"611809\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/611809-Statlib\">#Statlib</a>  can develop some API for ease of use on this..</p>",
        "id": 615529628,
        "sender_full_name": "Zixiao Jolene Wang",
        "timestamp": 1786331377
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"wink\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f609\" role=\"img\" title=\"wink\">:wink:</span> Would you like to formalize that exercise 5.3.7? And if you manage to generalize it to the most general space possible, maybe you can PR to mathlib?</p>",
        "id": 615529975,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1786331497
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(I tried loogle <code>HolderWith, 1 &lt; ?_</code> and <code>HolderOnWith, 1 &lt; ?_</code> but found nothing, so I assume it is missing)</p>",
        "id": 615530103,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1786331580
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>happy to! for the purpose of discussion here is the exercise; but to do this exercise one need to actually define holder space <code> C^{n,/gamma}</code> (picture shown above), not just one theorem <br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1814x312\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/v35wJM7KdLquWdDtaq7MdFd3/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/v35wJM7KdLquWdDtaq7MdFd3/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 615530240,
        "sender_full_name": "Zixiao Jolene Wang",
        "timestamp": 1786331676
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://mathoverflow.net/a/53127/\">https://mathoverflow.net/a/53127/</a><br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"748x408\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/OnjgjFYaUwK9TFDB6urjVvDg/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/OnjgjFYaUwK9TFDB6urjVvDg/image.png/840x560.webp\"><br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"661x325\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/rFOCtaFP-n_aHN8jAWKiI3xZ/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/rFOCtaFP-n_aHN8jAWKiI3xZ/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 615540614,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1786337905
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"872788\">Zixiao Jolene Wang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Definition.20of.20HolderWith.20with.20r.20.3E.201/near/615503696\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Hi all i found this <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/Holder.html\">HolderWith</a> where r is not  restricted to (0, 1] but ℝ≥0. However, the textbook convention differ for r &gt; 1:  since r &gt; 1 forces f to be constant see a simple proof here: <br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1906x1792\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/aYGkZSd76uoPe1H5igKoK_qz/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/aYGkZSd76uoPe1H5igKoK_qz/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think the commonly used space <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^{k,\\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"katex-html\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mpunct mtight\">,</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">γ</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> of functions with <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"katex-html\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span> derivatives which are <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"katex-html\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">γ</span></span></span></span>-Hölder should be defined separately at some point. As far as I know this is currently not in mathlib.</p>",
        "id": 615544906,
        "sender_full_name": "Joris van Winden",
        "timestamp": 1786340446
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There's a TODO in mathlib about defining <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> if you are in the mood.</p>",
        "id": 615611391,
        "sender_full_name": "Luigi Massacci",
        "timestamp": 1786364743
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Would we want <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^{k,0}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mpunct mtight\">,</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> to coincide with <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> or to be Hölder-Zygmund spaces here?</p>",
        "id": 615620709,
        "sender_full_name": "Joris van Winden",
        "timestamp": 1786367549
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess you want three different definitions: C^k, C^{k, a} (Hölder) and C^s_* (Hölder-Zygmund) with the theorem that if k + a = s and 0 &lt; a &lt; 1, then the later coincide</p>",
        "id": 615623097,
        "sender_full_name": "Moritz Doll",
        "timestamp": 1786368204
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I have a vague memory that Yury had so code on Hölder, but I could not find a PR</p>",
        "id": 615623605,
        "sender_full_name": "Moritz Doll",
        "timestamp": 1786368330
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There's this definition in the SardMoreira project: <a href=\"https://urkud.github.io/SardMoreira/docs/SardMoreira/ContDiffMoreiraHolder.html#ContDiffMoreiraHolderOn\">https://urkud.github.io/SardMoreira/docs/SardMoreira/ContDiffMoreiraHolder.html#ContDiffMoreiraHolderOn</a></p>",
        "id": 615656622,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1786376482
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for finding that! I just saw that it actually made it into mathlib as <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ContDiffPointwiseHolderAt#doc\">docs#ContDiffPointwiseHolderAt</a></p>",
        "id": 615736468,
        "sender_full_name": "Moritz Doll",
        "timestamp": 1786406419
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"rolling on the floor laughing\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f923\" role=\"img\" title=\"rolling on the floor laughing\">:rolling_on_the_floor_laughing:</span> Oh I didn't even realize it is in mathlib</p>",
        "id": 615748460,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1786411110
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yeah this is a point wise definition and we still need Hölder space i guess - we already have api of holder norm <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HolderNorm.html\">https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HolderNorm.html</a></p>",
        "id": 615919114,
        "sender_full_name": "Zixiao Jolene Wang",
        "timestamp": 1786472248
    }
]